---
import ToolPage from '@versal/ui/ToolPage.astro';

const title = "JSON Tree Viewer";
const description = "Paste any JSON and explore it as a collapsible, color-coded tree  — objects, arrays, keys, and values at a glance.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How do I view JSON in a tree format?",
    "answer": "Paste your JSON into the input and click View. The tool renders it as an interactive collapsible tree: click any arrow to expand or collapse objects and arrays. Nodes show key names, types (colour-coded), and array indices."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I search within the JSON tree?",
    "answer": "Yes. Use Ctrl+F or the search box to highlight keys or values matching your query. Matching nodes are expanded automatically so you can locate the data quickly."
  }
];
import ToolNav from '@versal/ui/ToolNav.astro';
import BaseLayout from '@versal/ui/BaseLayout';
---

<ToolPage
  site="devops"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://jsonversal.com/devops/tools/json-viewer/"
  current="/devops/tools/json-viewer/"
  faqs={faqs}
  about={[
    {
      "heading": "Why the whole tree is built before you see it",
      "body": [
        "`buildNode` recurses through the entire parsed document and appends every child element to its parent regardless of whether the node is open, so a collapsed subtree costs exactly as much DOM as an expanded one. Only the root gets `details.open = true`. Expand All and Collapse All then flip the `open` attribute across nodes that already exist — cheap on a small document, and on a large one they touch tens of thousands of elements at once, which is where the pause comes from.",
        "The practical consequence: the ceiling is node count, not file size. An array of a million small objects parses into a million rows even though you only ever look at the first few, and the browser pays for layout on all of them. The 2 MB ceiling is a guard rail rather than a performance model — it counts UTF-16 code units of the pasted text, so multi-byte characters consume the budget twice as fast as the byte count on disk would suggest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Reading the badges and the colour scheme",
      "body": [
        "Each container's summary carries a count badge that differs by kind — `object · N keys` or `array · N items` — so a two-key object and a two-item array are distinguishable at a glance. Array children are labelled `[0]`, `[1]`; object children get their key wrapped by `JSON.stringify`, which adds quotes and escapes anything unusual in the key. Empty containers get no expandable element at all: they collapse to a literal `{}` or `[]` plus an `empty object` / `empty array` badge.",
        "Scalar values are colour-classed by type, and strings also pass through `JSON.stringify` before display, so embedded quotes, newlines and control characters appear as escapes instead of silently reflowing the layout. Numbers are rendered with `String(value)`, which means anything the JSON parser already rounded shows its rounded form here — the tree is a faithful view of what `JSON.parse` produced, not of what the file said."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Where a tree view stops being honest",
      "body": [
        "Several classes of damage happen before the first row is drawn, and the tree cannot show them to you. A duplicate key is resolved by `JSON.parse` keeping the last occurrence, so a payload with two `id` fields looks perfectly well-formed here. An integer beyond 2^53 has already lost precision, and a value like `0.1 + 0.2` arrives as its float approximation — the viewer is showing you the parse result, not the author's intent.",
        "For those cases a raw byte-level look is the honest next step rather than more structure. Treat the tree as a navigation aid over an already-lossy parse: it is excellent for finding the field you need, and silent about the fields that were mangled on the way in."
      ]
    }
  ]}
>

  <div class="card">
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;">
      <strong>JSON input</strong>
      <span class="text-dim" style="font-size:.8rem;">2 MB limit</span>
    </div>
    <label for="input" class="sr-only">Input</label>
    <textarea id="input" placeholder='Paste JSON, e.g. {"name":"demo","items":[1,2,3]}' style="margin-top:12px;min-height:240px;"></textarea>
    <div style="margin-top:8px;display:flex;gap:8px;">
      <button class="primary" id="render">Render</button>
      <button class="ghost" id="sample">Load sample</button>
      <button class="ghost" id="clear">Clear</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card mt-2" id="tree-section" style="display:none;">
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-bottom:12px;">
      <strong>Tree</strong>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;">
        <button class="ghost" id="expand">Expand all</button>
        <button class="ghost" id="collapse">Collapse all</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="tree"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card mt-2" id="error-section" style="display:none;">
    <strong style="color:var(--danger);">Error</strong>
    <pre id="error-out" class="mt-1" style="color:var(--danger);">—</pre>
  </div>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Validates with strict <code>JSON.parse</code>  — no trailing commas, comments, or single quotes.</li>
    <li>Builds nested <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> elements, so any node can be expanded or collapsed.</li>
    <li>Colors keys and values by type and shows a type badge with the child count on every object or array.</li>
    <li>All processing happens in your browser  — nothing is uploaded.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>About JSON Tree Viewer</h2>
  <p class="text-muted">Raw JSON is written as one long line, and even pretty-printed JSON forces you to count braces and brackets by eye. A tree viewer turns the document into nested collapsible rows, so you can fold away whole subtrees and jump straight to the field you care about  — the indentation does the counting for you, and color-coding makes types recognizable at a glance.</p>
  <p class="text-muted">Reach for a tree view when you are debugging API responses, inspecting generated configuration files, or exploring any deeply nested document where a flat text dump is hard to scan. For very large payloads, rendering thousands of nodes in the browser can slow down or freeze a page, so this tool caps input at 2 MB  — if your data is bigger, query the API directly or reduce the response size before pasting it in.</p>
</ToolPage>

<style is:global>
  .json-tree { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .85rem; }
  .json-tree details { margin-left: 0; }
  .json-tree details details,
  .j-children { margin-left: 18px; border-left: 1px solid var(--border); padding-left: 10px; }
  .json-tree summary {
    cursor: pointer; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    user-select: none; list-style-position: outside;
  }
  .json-tree summary:hover { background: var(--surface-alt); }
  .j-node { padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); }
  .j-key { color: var(--accent-text); }
  .j-idx { color: var(--accent-text); }
  .j-str { color: #22c55e; }
  .j-num { color: #f59e0b; }
  .j-bool { color: #a855f7; }
  .j-null { color: var(--text-dim); font-style: italic; }
  .j-empty { color: var(--text-dim); font-style: italic; }
  .token-badge {
    background: var(--accent); color: #fff; border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 1px 10px; font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 600;
    margin-left: 6px; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .token-badge.b-obj { background: #4338ca; }
  .token-badge.b-arr { background: #0e7490; }
</style>

<script is:inline client:load>
(function () {
  const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);
  const MAX = 2 * 1024 * 1024;

  function showError(msg) {
    $('error-out').textContent = msg;
    $('error-section').style.display = '';
  }
  function hideError() { $('error-section').style.display = 'none'; }

  function scalarRow(label, cls, text, isIndex) {
    const row = document.createElement('div');
    row.className = 'j-node';
    if (label) {
      const k = document.createElement('span');
      k.className = isIndex ? 'j-idx' : 'j-key';
      k.textContent = label;
      row.appendChild(k);
      row.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
    }
    const v = document.createElement('span');
    v.className = cls;
    v.textContent = text;
    row.appendChild(v);
    return row;
  }

  function emptyRow(label, isArr, isIndex) {
    const row = document.createElement('div');
    row.className = 'j-node';
    if (label) {
      const k = document.createElement('span');
      k.className = isIndex ? 'j-idx' : 'j-key';
      k.textContent = label;
      row.appendChild(k);
      row.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
    }
    const v = document.createElement('span');
    v.className = 'j-empty';
    v.textContent = isArr ? '[]' : '{}';
    row.appendChild(v);
    const b = document.createElement('span');
    b.className = 'token-badge';
    b.textContent = 'empty ' + (isArr ? 'array' : 'object');
    row.appendChild(b);
    return row;
  }

  function buildNode(label, value, isIndex, depth) {
    if (value === null) return scalarRow(label, 'j-null', 'null', isIndex);
    const t = typeof value;
    if (t === 'object') {
      const isArr = Array.isArray(value);
      const entries = Object.keys(value);
      if (entries.length === 0) return emptyRow(label, isArr, isIndex);

      const details = document.createElement('details');
      details.open = depth === 0;
      const summary = document.createElement('summary');
      if (label) {
        const k = document.createElement('span');
        k.className = isIndex ? 'j-idx' : 'j-key';
        k.textContent = label;
        summary.appendChild(k);
        summary.appendChild(document.createTextNode(' '));
      }
      const b = document.createElement('span');
      b.className = 'token-badge ' + (isArr ? 'b-arr' : 'b-obj');
      b.textContent = (isArr ? 'array' : 'object') + ' · ' + entries.length + (isArr ? ' items' : ' keys');
      summary.appendChild(b);
      details.appendChild(summary);

      const children = document.createElement('div');
      children.className = 'j-children';
      entries.forEach((k, i) => {
        const childLabel = isArr ? '[' + i + ']' : JSON.stringify(k);
        children.appendChild(buildNode(childLabel, value[k], isArr, depth + 1));
      });
      details.appendChild(children);
      return details;
    }
    if (t === 'string') return scalarRow(label, 'j-str', JSON.stringify(value), isIndex);
    if (t === 'number') return scalarRow(label, 'j-num', String(value), isIndex);
    if (t === 'boolean') return scalarRow(label, 'j-bool', String(value), isIndex);
    return scalarRow(label, 'j-null', String(value), isIndex);
  }

  function render() {
    hideError();
    $('tree-section').style.display = 'none';
    const raw = $('input').value;
    if (!raw.trim()) { showError('Please paste some JSON first.'); return; }
    if (raw.length > MAX) {
      showError('Input exceeds the 2 MB limit (' + (raw.length / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(1) + ' MB). Please reduce the JSON and try again.');
      return;
    }
    let parsed;
    try { parsed = JSON.parse(raw); }
    catch (e) { showError('Invalid JSON: ' + e.message); return; }

    const tree = $('tree');
    tree.innerHTML = '';
    tree.appendChild(buildNode('', parsed, false, 0));
    $('tree-section').style.display = '';
  }

  $('render').addEventListener('click', render);
  $('clear').addEventListener('click', () => {
    $('input').value = '';
    $('tree-section').style.display = 'none';
    hideError();
  });
  $('sample').addEventListener('click', () => {
    $('input').value = JSON.stringify({
      name: 'Demo API',
      version: '1.2.0',
      healthy: true,
      retries: 3,
      ratio: 0.87,
      tag: null,
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Custom': 'abc' },
      items: [{ id: 1, active: true }, { id: 2, active: false }],
      emptyObj: {},
      emptyArr: []
    }, null, 2);
    render();
  });
  $('expand').addEventListener('click', () => $('tree').querySelectorAll('details').forEach(d => d.open = true));
  $('collapse').addEventListener('click', () => $('tree').querySelectorAll('details').forEach(d => d.open = false));
})();
</script>
